<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-superposition" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Superposition: Schrödinger's Cat as a Linear Combination</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The definition of a vector space says nothing about columns of numbers, so anything
      satisfying the axioms of <xref ref="def-vector-space" text="type-global" /> may be
      added and scaled exactly as vectors in <m>\R^n</m> are. We have already seen that
      matrices form such a space. In this section we take the two pictures below
      <mdash /> a cat alive in its box, and the same cat dead in the same box <mdash />
      read each one as a single vector, and form linear combinations of them. The
      pictures that result can be used classically (as opposed to quantum mechanically)
      to depict <term>Schrödinger's cat</term>.<fn>
        Schrödinger's cat is a famous thought experiment created by physicist Erwin
        Schrödinger in 1935. It explains quantum superposition <mdash /> the idea that a
        tiny particle can be in two different states at the same time until someone looks
        at it. In the story, a cat in a closed box is both alive and dead at once because
        its life depends on a hidden radioactive atom.
      </fn>
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing here is new mathematics. Every step is scalar multiplication, addition,
      span, basis, and coordinates, applied in a vector space whose vectors happen to be
      pictures. That is the point of generalizing: the machinery built for arrows in
      the plane runs unchanged on a space of dimension four hundred thousand.
    </p>
    <p>
      Interactive <term>Sage cells</term> appear throughout. Press <term>Evaluate</term>
      to run one in your browser, or copy the code into
      <url href="https://sagecell.sagemath.org" visual="sagecell.sagemath.org">the Sage
      cell server</url>. Each cell is safe to edit and re-run as often as you like; the
      lines marked <c># change me</c> are the ones worth experimenting with. The cells on
      this page share their variables and must be evaluated in order, so run each one as
      you reach it.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superposition-pictures">
    <title>A Picture Is a Vector</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m> denote the set of all <m>m \times n</m> matrices with
      real entries. The verification in
      <xref ref="eg-m22-vector-space" text="type-global" /> used nothing about the size
      <m>2 \times 2</m>, so it applies word for word to any fixed <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>:
      <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m> is a vector space. Its dimension is <m>mn</m>, since the
      matrices <m>E_{ij}</m> with a <m>1</m> in position <m>(i,j)</m> and zeros elsewhere
      span <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m> and are linearly independent: a linear combination
      <m>\sum_{i,j} c_{ij} E_{ij}</m> is the matrix whose <m>(i,j)</m> entry is
      <m>c_{ij}</m>, and that matrix is zero exactly when every <m>c_{ij}</m> is zero.
    </p>

    <p>
      A grayscale picture is an element of this space. As in
      <xref ref="sec-images-as-matrices" text="title" />, a picture <m>m</m> pixels tall
      and <m>n</m> pixels wide is the <m>m \times n</m> matrix whose <m>(i,j)</m> entry is
      the brightness of the pixel in row <m>i</m> and column <m>j</m>, on a scale where
      <m>0</m> is black and <m>255</m> is white. The two pictures in
      <xref ref="fig-cat-basis" text="global" /> are <m>780</m> pixels tall and
      <m>520</m> pixels wide, so each is a vector in <m>\mathbb{M}_{780,520}</m>, a space
      of dimension <m>405{,}600</m>. Call them
      <md>
        A = \text{the alive cat}, \qquad D = \text{the dead cat}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-cat-basis">
      <caption>
        Two vectors in <m>\mathbb{M}_{780,520}</m>. The two pictures share their box,
        their label, and their lighting, so the matrices <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> differ
        mainly where the cat is.
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="44% 44%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-alive">
          <caption>The alive cat: the matrix <m>A</m>.</caption>
          <image source="images/cat-alive.png">
            <shortdescription>A grayscale illustration of a cat sitting upright inside a wooden box labelled Schrodinger's Cat, beside a mechanism connected to a container marked with a skull and crossbones</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-dead">
          <caption>The dead cat: the matrix <m>D</m>.</caption>
          <image source="images/cat-dead.png">
            <shortdescription>The same box and mechanism in grayscale, with the cat lying motionless on the floor of the box and the container gone</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <p>
      These two vectors are linearly independent. If one were a scalar multiple of the
      other, say <m>A = cD</m>, then the same <m>c</m> would have to work at every pixel.
      The pixel in row <m>337</m> and column <m>256</m>, in the cat's left eye, has
      <m>A</m> entry <m>210</m> and <m>D</m> entry <m>75</m>, forcing
      <m>c = \frac{210}{75} = \frac{14}{5}</m>; the pixel in row <m>601</m> and column
      <m>301</m>, on the floor of the box, has <m>A</m> entry <m>68</m> and the same
      <m>D</m> entry <m>75</m>, forcing <m>c = \frac{68}{75}</m>. No single scalar does
      both, and reversing the roles of <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> fails for the same reason.
      So <m>\{A, D\}</m> is a linearly independent set of two vectors in a space of
      dimension <m>405{,}600</m>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superposition-combination">
    <title>The Superposition</title>

    <p>
      Quantum mechanics describes a system that can be found in either of two states by a
      linear combination of those states, called a <term>superposition</term>. Writing
      the two states as our two matrices, a superposition of the alive cat and the dead
      cat is
      <md>
        S = \alpha A + \beta D, \qquad \alpha^2 + \beta^2 = 1.
      </md>
      The scalars <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\beta</m> are the <term>amplitudes</term>. The
      normalization <m>\alpha^2 + \beta^2 = 1</m> is what makes the numbers
      <m>\alpha^2</m> and <m>\beta^2</m> add up to <m>1</m>, and physics reads them as the
      probability of finding the cat alive and the probability of finding it dead when the
      box is opened. Every such pair is <m>(\alpha, \beta) = (\cos t, \sin t)</m> for some
      angle <m>t</m>, so a single parameter sweeps through all the superpositions of these
      two states.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is one practical wrinkle before we can look at <m>S</m>. Its entries run as
      high as <m>255(\alpha + \beta)</m>, which for <m>t = 45^\circ</m> is about
      <m>360</m>, and no screen can display a brightness above <m>255</m>. So we display
      the scalar multiple
      <md>
        \widehat{S} = \frac{1}{\alpha + \beta}\left( \alpha A + \beta D \right)
        = \frac{\alpha}{\alpha + \beta} A + \frac{\beta}{\alpha + \beta} D,
      </md>
      whose coefficients are nonnegative and add to <m>1</m>, so that every entry lies
      between the corresponding entries of <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> and therefore between
      <m>0</m> and <m>255</m>. Rescaling changes nothing that matters: <m>S</m> and
      <m>\widehat{S}</m> span the same line through the origin, and physics likewise
      regards a state and any nonzero multiple of it as the same state.
      <xref ref="fig-cat-superposition" text="global" /> shows the result for five
      choices of <m>t</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-cat-superposition">
      <caption>
        The superposition <m>\alpha A + \beta D</m> with
        <m>(\alpha, \beta) = (\cos t, \sin t)</m>, rescaled for display, at
        <m>t = 0^\circ, 30^\circ, 45^\circ, 60^\circ, 90^\circ</m>. The captions give
        <m>\alpha^2</m>, the probability of finding the cat alive. Only the two ends of
        the row are pictures of a cat; everything between them is a vector in
        <m>\mathbb{M}_{780,520}</m> that happens to be drawable.
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="18% 18% 18% 18% 18%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-super-100">
          <caption><m>\alpha^2 = 1</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-super-100.png">
            <shortdescription>The alive cat alone</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-super-075">
          <caption><m>\alpha^2 = \frac{3}{4}</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-super-075.png">
            <shortdescription>The alive cat clearly visible with a faint ghost of the lying cat beneath it</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-super-050">
          <caption><m>\alpha^2 = \frac{1}{2}</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-super-050.png">
            <shortdescription>Both cats equally faint, the sitting cat and the lying cat overlapping in the same box</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-super-025">
          <caption><m>\alpha^2 = \frac{1}{4}</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-super-025.png">
            <shortdescription>The lying cat clearly visible with a faint ghost of the sitting cat above it</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-super-000">
          <caption><m>\alpha^2 = 0</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-super-000.png">
            <shortdescription>The dead cat alone</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <remark xml:id="rem-superposition-not-subspace">
      <title>Displayable Pictures Do Not Form a Subspace</title>
      <p>
        The rescaling above was forced on us, and it is worth saying why in the language
        of this chapter. The set
        <md>
          \mathcal{P} = \left\{ M \in \mathbb{M}_{mn} \; \middle| \;
            0 \leq m_{ij} \leq 255 \text{ for all } i, j \right\}
        </md>
        of pictures a screen can show is <em>not</em> a subspace of <m>\mathbb{M}_{mn}</m>.
        It contains <m>A</m> but not <m>2A</m>, so it fails closure under scalar
        multiplication, and it contains <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> but not <m>A + D</m>. It is
        a bounded box inside the vector space, not a subspace of it. The vector space is
        the larger, simpler object: the arithmetic is unrestricted, and only the last step
        <mdash /> looking at the answer <mdash /> requires the entries to be in range.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superposition-numbers">
    <title>The Arithmetic Behind One Blend</title>

    <p>
      The pictures are made of arithmetic, and the arithmetic is small enough to inspect.
      Take from each picture the patch around the alive cat's left eye <mdash /> the
      same corner of the picture magnified in
      <xref ref="sec-images-as-matrices" text="title" /> <mdash /> and average it in
      blocks down to eight rows and six columns. From the alive cat this gives
      <m>A_0</m>, and from the dead cat, whose box is empty in that corner, it gives
      <m>D_0</m>:
      <md>
        A_0 = \begin{bmatrix}
             65 \amp  63 \amp  80 \amp  84 \amp  81 \amp  79 \\
             73 \amp  86 \amp  90 \amp  88 \amp  90 \amp  87 \\
             81 \amp  89 \amp  86 \amp  90 \amp  93 \amp  86 \\
             89 \amp  68 \amp  76 \amp  69 \amp  95 \amp  91 \\
            103 \amp  91 \amp  78 \amp  87 \amp  91 \amp  91 \\
            100 \amp  99 \amp 108 \amp  83 \amp  99 \amp  94 \\
             90 \amp  97 \amp 104 \amp 100 \amp 102 \amp  86 \\
             84 \amp  94 \amp 110 \amp 107 \amp  70 \amp  63
        \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        D_0 = \begin{bmatrix}
            71 \amp 69 \amp 69 \amp 65 \amp 64 \amp 62 \\
            74 \amp 72 \amp 71 \amp 67 \amp 66 \amp 65 \\
            77 \amp 74 \amp 73 \amp 70 \amp 68 \amp 66 \\
            78 \amp 76 \amp 73 \amp 71 \amp 69 \amp 68 \\
            82 \amp 78 \amp 76 \amp 73 \amp 70 \amp 69 \\
            82 \amp 80 \amp 77 \amp 75 \amp 72 \amp 71 \\
            85 \amp 82 \amp 79 \amp 76 \amp 74 \amp 72 \\
            86 \amp 82 \amp 79 \amp 77 \amp 75 \amp 72
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
      The eye leaves <m>A_0</m> mottled: the pupil darkens the fourth row to <m>68</m>
      and <m>69</m>, and the bright iris below it pushes several entries past
      <m>100</m>. The empty
      wall leaves <m>D_0</m> a smooth gradient, every row a little lighter than the one
      above it and every column a little darker than the one to its left.
    </p>

    <p>
      The half-and-half superposition is <m>t = 45^\circ</m>, where
      <m>\alpha = \beta = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</m> and the rescaled matrix
      <m>\widehat{S}</m> is the average
      <md>
        \frac{1}{2}\left( A_0 + D_0 \right) = \begin{bmatrix}
            68 \amp 66 \amp 74 \amp 74 \amp 72 \amp 70 \\
            74 \amp 79 \amp 80 \amp 78 \amp 78 \amp 76 \\
            79 \amp 82 \amp 80 \amp 80 \amp 80 \amp 76 \\
            84 \amp 72 \amp 74 \amp 70 \amp 82 \amp 80 \\
            92 \amp 84 \amp 77 \amp 80 \amp 80 \amp 80 \\
            91 \amp 90 \amp 92 \amp 79 \amp 86 \amp 82 \\
            88 \amp 90 \amp 92 \amp 88 \amp 88 \amp 79 \\
            85 \amp 88 \amp 94 \amp 92 \amp 72 \amp 68
        \end{bmatrix},
      </md>
      rounded to integers. Every entry sits halfway between the two originals, which is
      why the eye in <xref ref="fig-cat-blocks" text="global" /> survives the blend at
      half strength. In <m>A_0</m> the pupil entry <m>68</m> lies <m>21</m> below the
      <m>89</m> directly above it; in the average, <m>72</m> lies <m>10</m> below
      <m>82</m>. The contrast, like everything else, has been halved.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-cat-blocks">
      <caption>
        The two blocks and their average, magnified so that the individual entries are
        visible. Each square is one entry, on the same scale as the pictures: <m>0</m>
        would be black and <m>255</m> would be white.
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="27% 27% 27%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-block-alive">
          <caption><m>A_0</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-block-alive.png">
            <shortdescription>A coarse grid of gray squares with a darker patch where the pupil is</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-block-mix">
          <caption><m>\frac{1}{2}(A_0 + D_0)</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-block-mix.png">
            <shortdescription>The same grid with the dark patch half as pronounced</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cat-block-dead">
          <caption><m>D_0</m></caption>
          <image source="images/cat-block-dead.png">
            <shortdescription>A coarse grid of gray squares shading smoothly from dark at the top to lighter at the bottom</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Enter the two blocks in Sage. The helper <c>show_pictures</c> defined here draws
      each matrix the way <xref ref="fig-cat-blocks" text="global" /> is drawn, with
      <c>vmin=0, vmax=255</c> pinning the ends of the scale so that a given number always
      produces the same shade.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        A0 = matrix(ZZ, [
        [ 65,  63,  80,  84,  81,  79],
        [ 73,  86,  90,  88,  90,  87],
        [ 81,  89,  86,  90,  93,  86],
        [ 89,  68,  76,  69,  95,  91],
        [103,  91,  78,  87,  91,  91],
        [100,  99, 108,  83,  99,  94],
        [ 90,  97, 104, 100, 102,  86],
        [ 84,  94, 110, 107,  70,  63]])
        D0 = matrix(ZZ, [
        [71, 69, 69, 65, 64, 62],
        [74, 72, 71, 67, 66, 65],
        [77, 74, 73, 70, 68, 66],
        [78, 76, 73, 71, 69, 68],
        [82, 78, 76, 73, 70, 69],
        [82, 80, 77, 75, 72, 71],
        [85, 82, 79, 76, 74, 72],
        [86, 82, 79, 77, 75, 72]])

        def show_pictures(*pictures):
            plots = [matrix_plot(M.change_ring(RDF), cmap='gray', vmin=0, vmax=255)
                     for M in pictures]
            graphics_array(plots, nrows=1).show(figsize=[3*len(plots), 3])

        show_pictures(A0, (A0 + D0)/2, D0)
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Now put the amplitudes under your own control. The cell below draws
      <m>\widehat{S}</m> for whatever angle you choose with the slider, and prints the
      amplitudes together with the two probabilities. Drag from one end to the other and
      watch one cat fade into the other.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        @interact
        def superpose(degrees=slider(0, 90, 5, default=45, label="angle t (degrees)")):
            t = RDF(degrees*pi/180)
            alpha, beta = cos(t), sin(t)
            print("alpha =", alpha.n(digits=4), "   beta =", beta.n(digits=4),
                  "   alpha^2 + beta^2 =", (alpha^2 + beta^2).n(digits=4))
            print("P(alive) =", (alpha^2).n(digits=4),
                  "   P(dead) =", (beta^2).n(digits=4))
            if abs(alpha + beta) &lt; 1e-6:
                print("alpha + beta is zero here, so there is no rescaling to make")
            else:
                S = (alpha*A0 + beta*D0)/(alpha + beta)  # rescaled so it can be drawn
                show_pictures(A0, S, D0)
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Two experiments are worth running in the cell above. First, change the upper end of
      the slider range from <c>90</c> to <c>180</c>. Past <m>90^\circ</m> the amplitude
      <m>\alpha</m> is negative, so the combination subtracts one picture from the other
      and the entries leave the displayable range; at <m>135^\circ</m> we have
      <m>\beta = -\alpha</m>, the rescaling would divide by zero, and the guard in the
      cell says so instead. The vector <m>\alpha A + \beta D</m> exists at every one of
      those angles. It is only the drawing that fails. Second, delete the division by
      <c>alpha + beta</c> and evaluate again: the entries now run past <m>255</m>, every
      bright region collapses to the same white, and
      <xref ref="rem-superposition-not-subspace" text="type-global" /> is on the screen.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superposition-plane">
    <title>The Plane Spanned by the Two Cats</title>

    <p>
      Collect all the linear combinations at once. By
      <xref ref="thm-span-is-subspace" text="type-global" />,
      <md>
        W = \operatorname{Span}\{A, D\}
        = \left\{ \alpha A + \beta D \; \middle| \; \alpha, \beta \in \R \right\}
      </md>
      is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{M}_{780,520}</m>, and since <m>\{A, D\}</m> is linearly
      independent it is a basis for <m>W</m>. So <m>W</m> is a two-dimensional subspace of
      a space of dimension <m>405{,}600</m>: a plane of cats, sitting inside an
      enormous space of pictures, containing every superposition of these two states and
      nothing else.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two dimensions means two coordinates. With <m>\mathcal{B} = \{A, D\}</m>, the
      coordinate vector of a superposition in the sense of
      <xref ref="def-coordinate-vector" text="type-global" /> is
      <md>
        [S]_{\mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix} \alpha \\ \beta \end{bmatrix} \in \R^2,
      </md>
      and by <xref ref="thm-coordinates-unique" text="type-global" /> no other pair of
      scalars produces the same picture. The coordinate mapping is an isomorphism from
      <m>W</m> to <m>\R^2</m>, so by <xref ref="rem-isomorphic-same" text="type-global" />
      the plane of cats <em>is</em> <m>\R^2</m> for every purpose that involves adding and
      scaling. The normalization <m>\alpha^2 + \beta^2 = 1</m> now has a shape: the
      normalized superpositions are the coordinate vectors on the unit circle, which is
      why one angle <m>t</m> was enough to list them all.
    </p>

    <p>
      A plane has more than one basis, and the alternative one matters here. Set
      <md>
        C_{+} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(A + D), \qquad
        C_{-} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(A - D).
      </md>
      These are the two states physics calls the <term>cat states</term>. Up to the
      rescaling that any display requires, the first is the half-and-half blend in the
      middle of <xref ref="fig-cat-superposition" text="global" /> and the second is the
      picture in <xref ref="fig-cat-difference" text="global" />, which records where the
      two pictures disagree. Since
      <md>
        A = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( C_{+} + C_{-} \right), \qquad
        D = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( C_{+} - C_{-} \right),
      </md>
      each basis is contained in the span of the other, so
      <m>\mathcal{C} = \{C_{+}, C_{-}\}</m> is another basis for the same plane <m>W</m>.
      The change of basis of <xref ref="def-change-of-basis" text="type-global" /> is
      carried out by
      <md>
        [S]_{\mathcal{C}} = P [S]_{\mathcal{B}}, \qquad
        P = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix},
      </md>
      a matrix satisfying <m>P^2 = I_2</m>, so the same <m>P</m> converts back again.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-cat-difference">
      <caption>
        The difference <m>A - D</m>, shifted by <m>128J</m> so that it can be displayed:
        mid-gray marks the pixels where the two pictures agree, and the departures from
        mid-gray are exactly where they do not. The box, the label, and the lighting
        cancel; the cat does not.
      </caption>
      <image source="images/cat-difference.png" width="44%">
        <shortdescription>An almost uniformly gray picture in which the box has vanished and two faint ghostly cats remain, one sitting and one lying</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      This is the whole content of the measurement puzzle, in coordinates. The alive cat
      is a basis vector of <m>\mathcal{B}</m> and has coordinates
      <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> there, but in <m>\mathcal{C}</m> its
      coordinates are <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>: a
      state that is definitely alive in one basis is an even superposition in the other.
      Being a superposition is not a property of the state. It is a property of the state
      <em>together with the basis</em> you chose to measure it against, exactly as a
      vector in <m>\R^2</m> has no coordinates until a basis is named.
    </p>

    <p>
      The cell below builds the two cat states out of the blocks, checks that <m>P</m>
      really does convert coordinates, and draws <m>C_{-}</m>. That last picture needs the
      shift used in <xref ref="fig-cat-difference" text="global" />, since a difference of
      two pictures has negative entries wherever the second is the brighter.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
        r = 1/sqrt(RDF(2))
        Cplus  = r*(A0 + D0)
        Cminus = r*(A0 - D0)
        P = matrix(RDF, [[r, r], [r, -r]])

        alpha, beta = RDF(0.6), RDF(0.8)          # change me: keep alpha^2 + beta^2 = 1
        S = alpha*A0 + beta*D0
        coordsB = vector(RDF, [alpha, beta])
        coordsC = P*coordsB
        print("coordinates in B:", coordsB)
        print("coordinates in C:", coordsC)
        print("same picture?",
              (coordsC[0]*Cplus + coordsC[1]*Cminus - S).norm() &lt; 1e-9)
        print("P^2 = I?", (P*P - identity_matrix(RDF, 2)).norm() &lt; 1e-9)

        J = ones_matrix(RDF, 8, 6)
        show_pictures(Cplus, 128*J + Cminus)
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Both checks compare a computed matrix with the one it should equal by measuring how
      far apart they are, rather than asking whether they are equal on the nose. Floating
      point arithmetic rounds at every step, so <c>==</c> can report <c>False</c> for two
      quantities that differ only in the sixteenth digit; the mathematics is exact even
      when the arithmetic is not. Change <c>alpha</c> and <c>beta</c> and evaluate again:
      the coordinates change in both bases, and both checks still pass.
    </p>

    <p>
      One detail of the last line is worth noticing. The blocks are dark enough that
      <m>C_{+}</m> can be drawn as it stands, but for the full pictures it cannot: the
      brightest entries of <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> are <m>214</m> and <m>184</m>, and
      <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(214 + 184)</m> is well over <m>255</m>. That is why
      <xref ref="fig-cat-superposition" text="global" /> shows
      <m>\frac{1}{2}(A + D)</m>, the rescaled version of <m>C_{+}</m>, rather than
      <m>C_{+}</m> itself.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-superposition-limits">
    <title>What the Pictures Do and Do Not Say</title>

    <p>
      The mathematics above is honest linear algebra, and it is worth being equally clear
      about where the analogy with physics stops.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>The real state space is complex.</term> Quantum states are vectors in a
            complex vector space, so the amplitudes are complex numbers with
            <m>|\alpha|^2 + |\beta|^2 = 1</m>. All ten axioms of
            <xref ref="def-vector-space" text="type-global" /> hold with
            <m>\mathbb{C}</m> in place of <m>\R</m>, and everything in this chapter
            carries over unchanged. Keeping the amplitudes real, as we did, keeps the
            pictures drawable; what it leaves out is the phase a complex amplitude
            carries, and the interference that comes with it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>A superposition is not a blurred picture.</term> Opening the box
            produces one cat or the other, never the blend in the middle of
            <xref ref="fig-cat-superposition" text="global" />. The faded pictures depict
            the coefficients, not what anyone would see.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Adding pictures is not adding states.</term> Averaging two pictures is
            a statement about brightness, and nothing physical happens when it is done.
            The combination means what it means here only because we declared these two
            pictures to stand for the two states; what you are looking at is a picture of
            <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\beta</m>, not of light leaving a box.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      What survives the caveats is the part this chapter is about. Two objects that are
      not columns of numbers <mdash /> two photographs, or two quantum states <mdash />
      can be added and scaled, and once that is granted, span, linear independence, basis,
      dimension, coordinates, and change of basis all apply without a word of
      modification. That is what the axioms of
      <xref ref="def-vector-space" text="type-global" /> bought us.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
